The row number tells us the last letter in that row. First, print spaces to center the row, then print letters from `A` up to the row letter and back down.

For `n = 3`, the rows are `A`, `ABA`, and `ABCBA`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern17(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int space = 1; space <= n - row; space++) {
                cout << " ";
            }

            for (char ch = 'A'; ch < 'A' + row; ch++) {
                cout << ch;
            }

            for (char ch = 'A' + row - 2; ch >= 'A'; ch--) {
                cout << ch;
            }

            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because every row can contain up to `2n - 1` characters.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output.

Instead of having separate loops for the upward and downward letters, this version treats each row as positions from `0` to `2 * row - 2`. The distance from the center tells which letter to print.

For row 4, the letter indexes are `0, 1, 2, 3, 2, 1, 0`, which gives `ABCDCBA`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern17(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int space = 1; space <= n - row; space++) {
                cout << " ";
            }

            int width = 2 * row - 1;
            int center = row - 1;

            for (int position = 0; position < width; position++) {
                int letterIndex = position <= center
                    ? position
                    : width - 1 - position;
                cout << char('A' + letterIndex);
            }

            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

This does not change any input because `n` is only read. It also avoids special handling for the middle letter.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` excluding the printed result.

Here the complete pattern is stored in a character grid first. Every row gets its centered letters by filling the left half and mirroring it into the right half. The grid makes the symmetry easy to see, but it uses more memory than printing row by row.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern17(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        int width = 2 * n - 1;
        char[][] grid = new char[n][width];

        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            int start = n - row - 1;
            int center = n - 1;

            for (int offset = 0; offset <= row; offset++) {
                char letter = (char) ('A' + offset);
                grid[row][start + offset] = letter;
                grid[row][center + offset] = letter;
            }
        }

        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            for (int column = 0; column < width; column++) {
                System.out.print(grid[row][column] == '\0'
                    ? " "
                    : grid[row][column]);
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

The check for `n <= 0` is the edge case here. Empty grid cells are printed as spaces, so the final shape remains centered.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` for filling and printing the grid.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the character grid.

Each recursive call handles one row. After printing that row, it calls itself for the next row. The row itself still uses loops for the spaces and the two letter directions.

The base condition stops the recursion after row `n`, so no extra line is printed.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern17(int n) {
        printRow(1, n);
    }

    private void printRow(int row, int n) {
        if (row > n) {
            return;
        }

        for (int space = 1; space <= n - row; space++) {
            System.out.print(" ");
        }

        for (char ch = 'A'; ch < 'A' + row; ch++) {
            System.out.print(ch);
        }

        for (char ch = (char) ('A' + row - 2); ch >= 'A'; ch--) {
            System.out.print(ch);
        }

        System.out.println();
        printRow(row + 1, n);
    }
}
```

For `n = 1`, the only output is `A`. The recursive stack has one active call for each row.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because of recursion.

